CAT = "fitrkBrakes"; SEG = "BRK"
# Brakes and speed governors, tyres and tyre-changing precautions, coupling systems (CE, DE).
# Road Traffic Act 729/2018 ss. 100, 104-106, 150-151.
Q = [
("An air brake system is split into separate circuits with a protection valve. What does that do if one circuit leaks?",
 "Keeps the others working", ["Empties every circuit at once", "Turns off the compressor", "Locks the steering"],
 "The multi-circuit protection valve isolates a leaking circuit so the other circuits keep their pressure and the vehicle can still be braked."),
("Why must water be kept out of the air brake system?",
 "It can freeze in valves", ["It weakens the tyres", "It makes the horn louder", "It raises the air pressure"],
 "Moisture in the lines freezes in winter and corrodes parts. The air dryer must work, and tanks without automatic drains must be drained."),
("Halfway up a hill near Kuopio, a buzzer sounds and the air pressure gauge drops into the red. What is the right response?",
 "Stop safely at once", ["Speed up to recharge", "Pump the brake pedal hard", "Ignore it until the depot"],
 "Low pressure means the service brakes may weaken and the spring brakes may come on. Stop where safe and find the cause."),
('You release the parking brake on an air-braked truck. What actually holds the spring brakes off while you drive?',
 'Air pressure in the chambers', ['A steel cable', 'Brake fluid pressure', "The engine's vacuum pump"],
 'Strong springs apply the parking brake; air pressure compresses them and holds the brakes off. If air is lost, the springs apply the brakes.'),
('You start a cold truck and the low-pressure buzzer sounds. How long should you wait before moving off?',
 'Until the warning stops and pressure is up', ['Ten seconds, then drive gently', 'Until the engine is warm', 'No wait if the load is light'],
 'Let the compressor fill the tanks until the warning goes off and the gauges show working pressure. Only then will the brakes work fully.'),
("On an icy road, why must you be careful with a strong retarder on an empty truck?",
 "It can lock the drive wheels", ["It heats the cab too much", "It drains the air tanks", "It works only on the trailer"],
 "A retarder or exhaust brake slows only the drive axle. On ice, with little weight on that axle, it can lock or skid the wheels and start a slide or jackknife, so use a low setting or switch it off."),
("Before a long steep descent, which gear should you choose with a loaded truck?",
 "A low gear, chosen at the top", ["The highest gear", "Neutral, to coast", "Any gear, then change down"],
 "Select a low gear before going down so the engine and endurance brake hold the speed. Changing down halfway may fail, and coasting or riding the brakes overheats them."),
("Your truck has ABS. What can it not do for you on a snowy road?",
 "Shorten the stopping distance", ["Stop the wheels locking", "Let you steer while braking", "Work with firm pedal pressure"],
 "ABS keeps the wheels from locking so you can steer while braking, but it cannot add grip. On snow and ice the stopping distance is still much longer, so slow down and keep a longer gap."),
("When must a truck combination have anti-lock brakes on every vehicle, under the Road Traffic Act?",
 "Over 22 metres unladen", ["When it carries passengers", "Only in the winter months", "When it has three axles"],
 "Section 151: if a truck combination is longer than 22.00 metres unladen, all its vehicles must have anti-lock brakes."),
("In winter, from November to March when conditions require, how deep must the tread be on a truck's driven axles?",
 "At least 5.0 mm", ["At least 3.0 mm", "At least 1.6 mm", "At least 8.0 mm"],
 "Section 105: trucks and buses must have winter tyres with at least 5.0 mm main groove depth on driven axles (except steering driven axles), and 3.0 mm on the other axles and the trailer."),
("In the winter tyre period, what tread is required on a truck's trailer axles?",
 "At least 3.0 mm", ["At least 5.0 mm", "At least 1.6 mm", "No rule applies"],
 "On axles other than the driven ones, and on all trailer axles, the main grooves must be at least 3.0 mm deep when winter tyres are required."),
("What does the Road Traffic Act say about tyres on the same axle?",
 "They must be alike", ["One may be a retread", "Mixing sizes is fine", "Only the left one counts"],
 "Tyres on one axle must match in size, construction and properties so the vehicle brakes and steers evenly."),
("A combination of more than 44 tonnes has under 18 percent of its mass on the driven axles in winter. What must the tractor have?",
 "A traction aid device", ["Snow chains on every axle", "A lower speed plate", "A second driver on board"],
 "Section 106: in that case the towing vehicle must have a device that improves moving off on slippery roads; a single-axle differential lock alone does not count."),
("You check the tyres on a truck with twin rear wheels. What should you look for between the twins?",
 "Trapped stones", ["Water in the tyre valves", "Faded paint on the rims", "The number of wheel nuts"],
 "Stones caught between twin tyres damage the sidewalls and can fly out at speed. Remove them during the walk-around."),
("Before changing a wheel on a heavy truck at the roadside, what comes first?",
 "Secure it and warn traffic", ["Remove all the wheel nuts", "Let air out of every tyre", "Jack up the whole rear axle"],
 "Stop where safe, apply the parking brake, chock the wheels, use hazard lights and the warning triangle where needed, then work on firm ground."),
("Where must you never be while a truck is held up only by a jack?",
 "Under the vehicle", ["Beside the jack handle", "In the cab", "Behind the trailer"],
 "A jack can slip. Never put any part of your body under a vehicle supported only by a jack; use axle stands."),
("After a wheel has been changed, what should be done once the truck has been driven a short way?",
 "Retighten the wheel nuts", ["Change the engine oil", "Swap the tyres side to side", "Reset the tachograph"],
 "Wheel nuts can settle. Check them with a torque wrench after the distance the maker gives, usually within the first hundred kilometres."),
("A trailer has a self-steering axle with a lock the driver can set. What does the Road Traffic Act require above 60 km/h?",
 "Locked straight", ["It must be lifted", "Nothing special", "Free to self-steer"],
 "Section 100: a self-steering axle with a lock must be kept locked straight at speeds above 60 km/h."),
("You have reversed under a semi-trailer and heard the coupling engage. How do you confirm it has really locked?",
 "Check the lock, tug test", ["Only listen for the click", "Drive off and brake hard", "Check the trailer lights"],
 "Check the locking handle and safety catch, look that the jaws are closed round the kingpin, then try to pull forward gently with the trailer brakes on."),
("Under the common ISO colour code, what does the red air coupling head on a trailer carry?",
 "Supply (emergency) line", ["Control (service) line", "The air suspension line", "The electrical feed"],
 "Red is the supply line that charges the trailer reservoir; yellow is the control line for the service brakes."),
("You are about to uncouple a semi-trailer in a soft gravel yard. What must you see to before lowering the landing legs?",
 "Firm ground or base plates", ["An empty trailer", "A running engine", "A wet surface"],
 "The landing legs must stand on firm, level ground; on soft ground use boards or plates under them, or the trailer may sink and tip when the tractor drives away."),
("What does the law require of the brakes and lights of vehicles coupled together?",
 "They must work together", ["Only the tractor's must work", "Trailer lights are optional by day", "Trailer brakes may be off"],
 "Section 150: the trailer's brakes and lights must work as required, and the electric and air brake systems of the vehicles must be matched."),
("A truck combination may have only one towed vehicle unless the law says otherwise. Which long combination is allowed?",
 "Truck, dolly and semi-trailer", ["Truck and three full trailers", "Bus and a semi-trailer", "Car towing two trailers"],
 "Section 151 lists truck combinations over 22 metres, including a truck with a dolly and semi-trailer, or a full trailer."),
("Which trailer may be coupled to a bus?",
 "A full trailer", ["A semi-trailer", "Two trailers in a row", "Nothing over 750 kg"],
 "Section 151: a centre-axle trailer or a full trailer may be coupled to a bus. Driving it needs category DE when the trailer is over 750 kg."),
("Why should a trailer's parking brake be applied before you uncouple it?",
 "So it cannot roll away", ["To charge its battery", "To lower the landing legs", "To save air"],
 "When the air lines are disconnected the trailer must be held still. Apply its parking brake and check it on level, firm ground."),
("After driving through deep slush in sub-zero weather, what may happen to the brakes?",
 "Ice may weaken them", ["They grip much harder", "They become self-adjusting", "Nothing ever changes"],
 "Ice and slush can build up on brake parts. Test the brakes gently when it is safe, and leave more room ahead."),
("You check brake slack adjusters in a walk-around and one pushrod travels much further than the others. What does it suggest?",
 "That brake needs adjusting", ["The tyre is under-inflated", "The air dryer is new", "It is normal on any axle"],
 "Uneven pushrod travel means a brake out of adjustment, which cuts braking force and pulls the vehicle. Have it fixed before driving."),
("Why should you not leave a heavy vehicle on the service brake alone when you step out?",
 "Air can leak away", ["It wears the tyres", "It drains the battery", "It locks the steering"],
 "The service brake depends on air pressure that can leak away. Always apply the spring parking brake before leaving the cab."),
]
